/* Lesson quizzes — batch 5 */
window.LQ=window.LQ||{};
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p10m1":[
 {id:"p10m1q1", q:"A 915 MHz LoRa gateway's radio puts out 22 dBm conducted, the coax run loses 2 dB, and the antenna is 6 dBi. What is its EIRP, and roughly how many milliwatts is that?", options:["28 dBm, about 630 mW","26 dBm, about 400 mW","22 dBm, about 160 mW, since antenna gain adds no power","30 dBm, about 1 W"], answer:1, why:"EIRP = 22 − 2 + 6 = 26 dBm, and 26 = 20 + 3 + 3, so 100 mW doubled twice, about 400 mW. Antenna gain counts toward EIRP because it focuses power toward the far end; 28 dBm forgets to subtract the cable loss."},
 {id:"p10m1q2", q:"A client hears an AP at −78 dBm on a 40 MHz channel. The receiver's noise figure is 6 dB. Using thermal noise of −174 dBm/Hz, what is the SNR?", options:["17 dB","20 dB","23 dB","14 dB"], answer:3, why:"Noise in 40 MHz = −174 + 10 log10(40,000,000) ≈ −174 + 76 = −98 dBm; the 6 dB noise figure lifts it to −92 dBm, so SNR = −78 − (−92) = 14 dB. 17 dB uses the 20 MHz floor, but doubling the channel width adds 3 dB of noise."},
 {id:"p10m1q3", q:"A link runs 1024-QAM (10 bits per symbol) with only a little SNR to spare. A new interior wall costs 6 dB. Using the lesson's rule of roughly 3 dB of SNR per bit per symbol, what will rate selection most likely settle on?", options:["256-QAM, 8 bits per symbol","64-QAM, 6 bits per symbol","16-QAM, 4 bits per symbol","1024-QAM still, since the link stays connected"], answer:0, why:"Losing 6 dB at about 3 dB per bit removes two bits per symbol: 10 becomes 8, which is 256-QAM. 64-QAM would need a 12 dB loss. Staying connected is not the test; each constellation needs its own SNR, and rate selection steps down as SNR falls."},
 {id:"p10m1q4", q:"A 2 km shop-to-house link at 2.4 GHz has clear visual line of sight, yet it runs several dB below its link budget. At the midpoint a barn roof sits 3 m below the straight line between the antennas. What is the best diagnosis and fix?", options:["Nothing blocks the view, so it must be co-channel interference; change channels","The path is clear, so raise transmit power to the 1 W conducted limit","The roof intrudes into the first Fresnel zone; raise the antennas","2.4 GHz cannot reach 2 km; move the link to 900 MHz so it passes through the barn"], answer:2, why:"Radius ≈ 8.66 × √(2/2.4) ≈ 7.9 m, and at least 60% (about 4.7 m) should be clear. A roof 3 m below the line blocks part of the zone and costs several dB even with clear sight. More power or a new channel ignores the obstruction."},
 {id:"p10m1q5", q:"Someone swaps the 3 dBi antenna on a certified 2.4 GHz access point for a 14 dBi omni and runs the radio at 30 dBm conducted. Which statement is correct under FCC Part 15?", options:["It is legal: conducted power is still within the 1 W limit","Illegal: EIRP becomes 44 dBm, above the 36 dBm limit","It is legal because an antenna is passive and cannot add power","It is legal as long as the device accepts any interference it receives"], answer:1, why:"EIRP = 30 + 14 = 44 dBm, eight dB over the 36 dBm that 15.247 allows (1 W with up to a 6 dBi antenna). Conducted power alone is not the test; the regulator counts EIRP, and 15.203 exists because antenna swaps change it."}
],
"p10m2":[
 {id:"p10m2q1", q:"A 2×2 Wi-Fi 6 laptop on an 80 MHz channel (980 data subcarriers, 13.6 µs per symbol including guard) is running 256-QAM with rate-3/4 coding. What PHY rate does it report?", options:["432 Mb/s","1,153 Mb/s","865 Mb/s","1,201 Mb/s"], answer:2, why:"980 × 8 bits × 3/4 = 5,880 bits per 13.6 µs ≈ 432 Mb/s per stream, and two spatial streams double it to about 865 Mb/s. 432 forgets the second stream, 1,153 skips the coding rate, and 1,201 is the 1024-QAM 5/6 maximum."},
 {id:"p10m2q2", q:"On one channel, ten laptops each send a 1,500-byte frame at 400 Mb/s, and a distant barcode scanner sends one 1,500-byte frame at 6 Mb/s. Ignoring overhead, roughly what share of that payload airtime does the scanner use?", options:["About 87%","About 9%, one device in eleven","About 50%","About 1.5%, since it is 67 times slower"], answer:0, why:"The scanner's frame takes 12,000 bits ÷ 6 Mb/s = 2 ms; each laptop frame takes 30 µs, so 0.3 ms for all ten. 2 of 2.3 ms is about 87%. Wi-Fi shares airtime, not bits, so counting devices (9%) badly understates a slow client's cost."},
 {id:"p10m2q3", q:"A client's 5 GHz AP on channel 100 sits two miles from an airport. Twice a day every client drops for about a minute and then reconnects. What is the most likely cause and fix?", options:["Co-channel interference from neighbours; widen the channel to 160 MHz for more capacity","Sticky clients holding a distant AP; enable 802.11r so they roam faster","Microwave oven interference; move the AP to 2.4 GHz channel 6","DFS radar events force the AP off channel; move to a non-DFS channel"], answer:3, why:"Channel 100 is in U-NII-2C, shared with radar. On a detection the AP must leave within 10 seconds, and a new DFS channel needs a 60-second listen, matching the minute-long drops. Wider channels or roaming settings do not change the radar rule."},
 {id:"p10m2q4", q:"An office runs WPA3-Enterprise with PEAP-MSCHAPv2. To stop a pop-up, staff laptops were set not to validate the RADIUS server's certificate. What is the real risk, and the fix?", options:["None; the TLS tunnel protects the password whichever server answers","A rogue AP can collect passwords; require server-certificate validation","Offline guessing of the shared passphrase; lengthen it to 16 or more random characters","Forged deauthentication frames knock laptops off; enabling PMF removes the risk"], answer:1, why:"PEAP protects the password only inside a tunnel to a server the client has verified. Skip validation and any network answering with the same SSID terminates the tunnel and receives the credentials. 802.1X has no shared passphrase, and PMF does not authenticate the RADIUS server."},
 {id:"p10m2q5", q:"You move a casting device to the IoT VLAN. The firewall lets Trusted start connections to IoT and returns replies statefully, yet phones on Trusted no longer see the device in their cast menu. What is missing?", options:["A rule letting IoT devices start connections into the Trusted VLAN","Client isolation on the IoT SSID","An mDNS reflector so discovery multicasts cross between the VLANs","WPA3 on the IoT SSID, because the device refuses WPA2 clients"], answer:2, why:"Discovery uses multicast DNS, which never leaves its own VLAN, so the phone cannot find the device even though unicast connections are allowed. A reflector forwards only discovery. Letting IoT open connections into Trusted would break the segmentation the VLAN exists for."}
],
"p10m3":[
 {id:"p10m3q1", q:"A BLE sensor's advertising event costs 12 µC, it sleeps at 3 µA, and its firmware advertises every 500 ms. Roughly how long will a 225 mAh CR2032 last?", options:["About 1.7 years, averaging 15 µA","About 11 months, averaging 27 µA","About 2.9 years, averaging 9 µA","About 8.6 years, since it sleeps at 3 µA"], answer:1, why:"Two events per second × 12 µC = 24 µA, plus 3 µA of sleep, averages 27 µA; 225 mAh ÷ 0.027 mA ≈ 8,300 hours, about 11.4 months. The 15 µA answer counts one event per second, but a 500 ms interval doubles the event rate."},
 {id:"p10m3q2", q:"A Pi subscribes to a BLE logger that sends one notification per connection event using the default ATT MTU of 23 bytes. The connection interval is 30 ms. What application throughput should you expect?", options:["About 1 Mb/s, the LE 1M PHY rate","About 6.1 kb/s, 23 bytes per event","About 1.3 Mb/s, BLE's practical maximum","About 5.3 kb/s, 20 bytes per event"], answer:3, why:"A 23-byte MTU leaves 20 bytes of payload after the 3-byte ATT header: 20 bytes every 30 ms is about 667 B/s, or 5.3 kb/s. The PHY rate and the 1.3 Mb/s figure need data length extension, a larger MTU and the 2M PHY, none of which is in use."},
 {id:"p10m3q3", q:"You are designing a BLE door lock with no screen or keypad. Which pairing design matches the lesson's advice for something that unlocks?", options:["Add an NFC out-of-band step so it can do authenticated LE Secure Connections pairing","Just Works is enough, because LE Secure Connections' ECDH also stops a man-in-the-middle","Rely on resolvable private addresses rotating every 15 minutes to keep attackers out","Use LE Legacy pairing with a fixed passkey printed on the box"], answer:0, why:"With no input or output the lock can only do Just Works, which encrypts but cannot detect a man-in-the-middle during pairing; out-of-band pairing over NFC adds authentication. ECDH defeats passive listeners only, and rotating addresses give privacy, not access control."},
 {id:"p10m3q4", q:"Your bleak script connects to a heart-rate strap, finds service 0x180D and characteristic 0x2A37, and waits, but no readings ever arrive. What is the most likely missing step?", options:["Poll the characteristic faster, since the strap only answers clients that read every interval","Switch to the LE Coded PHY to improve the link budget","Enable notifications by writing 0x0001 to the characteristic's CCCD (0x2902)","Read Battery Level (0x2A19) first, because the strap sends data only after a battery read"], answer:2, why:"Heart Rate Measurement is a notify characteristic: the peripheral pushes values only after the client writes 0x0001 to its Client Characteristic Configuration Descriptor, which bleak's start_notify does. The link already works, so changing the PHY does not fix a missing subscription."},
 {id:"p10m3q5", q:"A laptop's Bluetooth headset crackles whenever the laptop pulls a large download over 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi, and is fine otherwise. What explains it, and what fixes it?", options:["BLE's advertising channels sit on top of Wi-Fi channel 6; move the AP to channel 1","The combo chip shares its antenna and airtime; move the laptop to 5 or 6 GHz","Adaptive frequency hopping causes the collisions; turn it off in the headset settings","The headset is Class 2; a 100 mW Class 1 headset will overpower the Wi-Fi"], answer:1, why:"Wi-Fi and Bluetooth on a combo chip share an antenna and arbitrate airtime internally, so heavy 2.4 GHz Wi-Fi starves the audio stream; 5 or 6 GHz removes the contention. Hopping is what lets them coexist, and the advertising channels sit in the gaps around channels 1, 6 and 11."}
],
"p10m4":[
 {id:"p10m4q1", q:"You want a Pi 5 to try NVMe first, then USB, then the SD card, then restart and try again. BOOT_ORDER is read right to left (1 = SD, 4 = USB, 6 = NVMe, f = restart). Which value do you set?", options:["0x641f","0xf416","0xf146","0xf461"], answer:2, why:"Read right to left, 0xf146 tries 6 (NVMe), then 4 (USB), then 1 (SD), then f (restart). 0xf416 also starts with NVMe but tries SD before USB, and 0x641f is the wanted order written left to right, which puts restart first."},
 {id:"p10m4q2", q:"A Pi 5 runs from a 5.0 V phone charger through a thin cable with 0.25 Ω round-trip resistance and draws 2.4 A under load. What voltage reaches the board, and what will vcgencmd get_throttled show?", options:["About 4.75 V, safely above the low-voltage threshold, so 0x0","About 4.4 V, so under-voltage bits 0x1 and 0x10000","About 5.0 V, because USB-C regulates at the device end, so 0x0","About 4.4 V, which shows only as the soft temperature bit 0x8"], answer:1, why:"Drop = 0.25 Ω × 2.4 A = 0.6 V, so 5.0 V arrives as 4.4 V, below the roughly 4.63 V low-voltage trip, setting 0x1 now and 0x10000 since boot. The 4.75 V answer subtracts the resistance as if it were volts instead of multiplying it by the current."},
 {id:"p10m4q3", q:"A Python sensor service on a Pi crashes a few nights a week, and once the whole board hung until someone power-cycled it. Which change addresses both failures?", options:["Start the script from rc.local with nohup so it keeps running after logout","A cron job that reboots the Pi every night at 3 a.m.","Restart=on-failure alone, since systemd also recovers a hung kernel","A systemd unit with Restart=on-failure, plus the hardware watchdog"], answer:3, why:"Restart=on-failure restarts a crashed process within seconds, but a hung kernel stops systemd too; only the hardware watchdog, which reboots the board when systemd stops feeding it, covers that. Nightly reboots and nohup neither restart a crash promptly nor recover a hang."},
 {id:"p10m4q4", q:"A tutorial script using RPi.GPIO ran fine on a Pi 4 but fails on a new Pi 5, though the wiring is identical. What is the cause and fix?", options:["Pi 5 GPIO runs at 5 V, so the script needs new voltage thresholds","I2C must be enabled with dtparam before any GPIO pin works","GPIO now sits on RP1 behind PCIe; port the script to gpiozero or libgpiod","System Python is too old; install RPi.GPIO with sudo pip outside a virtual environment"], answer:2, why:"RPi.GPIO writes BCM283x registers directly, but on a Pi 5 the header is driven by RP1 across a PCIe link, so those registers are not there. gpiozero (built on lgpio) and libgpiod work. The pins are still 3.3 V, and installing into system Python is discouraged since Bookworm."},
 {id:"p10m4q5", q:"A client wants to manage a Home Assistant Pi from their phone while travelling. Which setup follows the lesson's guidance?", options:["WireGuard or Tailscale, no inbound ports, key-only SSH","Forward ports 22 and 8123 from the router and use a strong SSH password","Move SSH to port 2222 and forward it, since scanners only probe port 22","Put the Pi in the router's DMZ so every port is reachable and nothing breaks"], answer:0, why:"A VPN overlay reaches the Pi without exposing any port to the internet's scanners, and key-only SSH removes password guessing. Forwarding ports, even on a non-standard number, still publishes the service to automated scanning, and a DMZ exposes everything."}
],
"p10m5":[
 {id:"p10m5q1", q:"An RTL-SDR is set to 2.0 MS/s complex IQ sampling with 8-bit I and Q values. How wide a slice of spectrum does it show, and how large is a two-minute IQ recording?", options:["1.0 MHz wide, because Nyquist halves the rate; 240 MB","2.0 MHz wide; 480 MB","2.0 MHz wide; 240 MB","4.0 MHz wide, since I and Q each add 2 MHz; 960 MB"], answer:1, why:"Complex IQ sampling at fs captures fs of bandwidth, so 2.0 MHz. Each sample is two bytes (I and Q): 2.0 MS/s × 2 B × 120 s = 480 MB. The halving rule applies to real-valued sampling, and 240 MB forgets that every sample carries both I and Q."},
 {id:"p10m5q2", q:"You try to receive a weak NOAA Weather Radio signal with an RTL-SDR while a strong FM broadcast tower stands 2 km away. At high gain the noise floor jumps, false signals appear across the waterfall and the weather audio gets worse. What should you do first?", options:["Lower the gain; strong signals are overloading the 8-bit ADC","Raise the gain further so the weak signal climbs above the false ones","Widen the sample rate so the FM station falls inside the window and stops aliasing","Fit a longer antenna, since false signals mean the antenna is mismatched"], answer:0, why:"An ideal 8-bit converter spans about 50 dB, so a strong nearby station at high gain overloads it, lifting the floor and creating spurious signals that bury weak ones. Backing the gain off restores headroom; more gain makes the overload worse."},
 {id:"p10m5q3", q:"An ADS-B antenna sits 16 m up. Using radio horizon ≈ 4.12 × (√h1 + √h2) km with heights in metres, about how far away can it hear an aircraft flying at 6,400 m, with nothing blocking the view?", options:["About 16 km, the antenna's own horizon","About 26,000 km","No fixed limit; range depends on receiver gain","About 346 km"], answer:3, why:"√16 = 4 and √6,400 = 80, so 4.12 × 84 ≈ 346 km. ADS-B range is a line-of-sight horizon problem: beyond that distance the Earth's curvature blocks the path, and no receiver gain hears around it. 26,000 km comes from forgetting the square roots."},
 {id:"p10m5q4", q:"A dental office uses 125 kHz EM4100-style badges. The owner has read that such badges can be copied and asks for your recommendation. Which is sound?", options:["Move to MIFARE Classic cards, which add encryption to every badge","Keep the badges; 125 kHz tags work only at a few centimetres, so copying is impractical","Migrate to AES-based credentials such as DESFire EV3 or Seos, with badge logs","Keep the badges but reissue new ID numbers to everyone every year"], answer:2, why:"An EM4100-style tag broadcasts a fixed ID with no cryptography, so it is a serial number, not a key, and reissuing numbers changes nothing. MIFARE Classic's Crypto-1 cipher was publicly broken in 2008, so the fix is AES with mutual authentication, plus logging."},
 {id:"p10m5q5", q:"A Flipper owner installs third-party firmware that removes the regional transmit limits, then transmits on a sub-GHz frequency not permitted in the US. Which statement is accurate?", options:["The user is liable; it breaks Part 15 whatever the firmware","It is lawful because the Flipper carries an FCC equipment authorization","Only the firmware's author is liable, since the user did not write the code","It is lawful as long as each transmission is a short burst under 15.231"], answer:0, why:"The official firmware limits transmission to frequencies permitted in the provisioned region; removing that limit moves the whole legal burden to the user. The device's authorization covers it as certified, not a modified unit transmitting where Part 15 does not allow it."}
],
"p10m6":[
 {id:"p10m6q1", q:"A deck uses a 3S3P pack of 2.5 Ah 18650 cells (3.6 V nominal each) feeding the Pi through a 90%-efficient buck converter. The deck measures 15 W. Ignoring the low-voltage cutoff reserve, about how long does it run?", options:["About 5.4 hours","About 1.6 hours","About 4.9 hours","About 2.3 hours"], answer:2, why:"3S gives 3 × 3.6 = 10.8 V and 3P gives 3 × 2.5 = 7.5 Ah, so 81 Wh; 90% conversion leaves about 73 Wh, and 73 ÷ 15 ≈ 4.9 hours. 5.4 hours ignores the converter loss, and 1.6 hours forgets that series cells add voltage."},
 {id:"p10m6q2", q:"A Pi 5 deck runs from a bank labelled '100 W USB-C PD'. The Pi warns about its supply and the USB SSD keeps dropping out. What explains it, and what is a sound fix?", options:["The bank is faulty, since 100 W at 5 V would be 20 A; buy a 140 W bank","PD's 5 V profiles stop at 3 A, so USB is capped; move the SSD to NVMe","Set usb_max_current_enable=1 so this bank delivers 1.6 A to the USB ports","Feed 5 V from the bank into the GPIO header pins to bypass PD negotiation"], answer:1, why:"The 100 W is offered at 20 V; at 5 V, standard PD stops at 3 A, so the Pi sees a 15 W source and limits USB to 600 mA. NVMe draws from the board's rails, not the USB budget. Telling the firmware it has 5 A when it does not invites brownouts."},
 {id:"p10m6q3", q:"You need a deck that runs Linux tools (Python scripts, a serial terminal) as a text-only field logger for 10 or more hours from a 20 Wh pack. Which compute board fits best?", options:["A Raspberry Pi 5 with 8 GB, for headroom","An x86 mini PC, for full driver support","A Raspberry Pi Pico, since milliwatts last longest","A Pi Zero 2 W, drawing about 1–2 W"], answer:3, why:"At 1–2 W the Zero 2 W gets 10–20 hours from 20 Wh and still runs Linux. A Pi 5 at 8–12 W manages about two hours and an x86 box less. A Pico sips power but has no Linux to run the tools the job needs."},
 {id:"p10m6q4", q:"A sealed case has a thermal resistance of about 5 °C per watt. The deck inside draws 9 W on a 30 °C afternoon. Roughly what is the air temperature inside, and what follows?", options:["About 75 °C; the SoC will soon throttle, so add a fan and vents","About 39 °C; the case adds about 1 °C per watt","About 45 °C, comfortably below any throttle point","About 75 °C, but moving to a larger sealed case fixes it without airflow"], answer:0, why:"Rise ≈ 9 W × 5 °C/W = 45 °C, added to the 30 °C ambient, gives about 75 °C inside, so the SoC soon throttles. 45 °C forgets the ambient. A bigger box without airflow still traps nearly every watt as heat."},
 {id:"p10m6q5", q:"A builder makes a 4S2P pack from 3.5 Ah cells (3.6 V nominal) and wants to fly with it. Under US airline rules, what applies?", options:["It is about 25 Wh, so it flies in carry-on without question","It is under 100 Wh, so carry-on without approval","About 100.8 Wh, so carry-on only with airline approval","Homemade lithium packs are banned from aircraft entirely"], answer:2, why:"4S2P gives 4 × 3.6 = 14.4 V and 2 × 3.5 = 7.0 Ah, so 100.8 Wh, inside the 100–160 Wh band that needs airline approval; spare packs never go in checked baggage. The 25 Wh answer ignores the series voltage."}
],
"p10m7":[
 {id:"p10m7q1", q:"GPIO 22 must switch a 5 V relay coil that draws 100 mA through a 2N2222. Using the lesson's rule of about one-twentieth of the collector current into the base and a 0.7 V base-emitter drop, which base resistor do you pick?", options:["About 26 Ω, sized for the 100 mA coil current","About 470 Ω, giving roughly 5.5 mA of base current","About 5.2 kΩ, giving 0.5 mA of base current","No resistor; the base draws only the current it needs"], answer:1, why:"Base current ≈ 100 mA ÷ 20 = 5 mA, so Rb = (3.3 − 0.7) ÷ 0.005 = 520 Ω; round down to 470 Ω to keep the transistor saturated (5.5 mA, inside the 16 mA pin limit). 26 Ω sizes for the coil and would demand about 100 mA from the pin."},
 {id:"p10m7q2", q:"Your I2C bus at 400 kHz has about 150 pF of capacitance after adding a ribbon cable. Using tr ≈ 0.8473 × Rp × C ≤ 300 ns, and a minimum of about 970 Ω set by the 3 mA sink limit, what pull-up range works?", options:["About 970 Ω to 2.4 kΩ","About 970 Ω to 3.5 kΩ","About 2.4 kΩ to 10 kΩ","10 kΩ or more, to save power"], answer:0, why:"Rp ≤ 300 ns ÷ (0.8473 × 150 pF) ≈ 2.36 kΩ, and the sink limit sets the floor near 970 Ω, so roughly 970 Ω to 2.4 kΩ; the Pi's 1.8 kΩ still fits. 3.5 kΩ is the 100 pF answer, and larger pull-ups make edges too slow for fast mode."},
 {id:"p10m7q3", q:"A 240 W PoE+ switch powers Wi-Fi APs that each request 20 W over LLDP. How many APs can it power if it reserves by class (30 W per PoE+ port), and how many if it allocates by LLDP request?", options:["9 by class, using 25.5 W per port; 12 by LLDP","8 either way, since PoE+ always means 30 W","8 by class; 12 by LLDP request","12 by class; 8 by LLDP request"], answer:2, why:"Class budgeting reserves the full 30 W a PoE+ port can source: 240 ÷ 30 = 8. LLDP lets each AP ask for what it needs: 240 ÷ 20 = 12. The 25.5 W figure is what reaches the device after cable loss, not what the switch reserves."},
 {id:"p10m7q4", q:"A Pi switches a 12 V solenoid through a logic-level MOSFET. It works for a few days, then the MOSFET fails shorted, and the replacement fails the same way. What is the most likely missing part?", options:["A 100 kΩ pull-up on the gate so it idles high","A level shifter, because the MOSFET gate needs 5 V logic","A 10 kΩ series gate resistor to slow the switching edges","A flyback diode across the solenoid coil"], answer:3, why:"A solenoid is inductive: when the MOSFET turns off, the collapsing field produces a spike of tens of volts that destroys the switch over time, and a flyback diode clamps it. Gate resistors and level shifting change how it switches, not the inductive spike."},
 {id:"p10m7q5", q:"A sensor's datasheet lists an absolute maximum VDD of 4.0 V and recommended VDD of 1.7–3.6 V. A teammate wants to run it from a 3.8 V rail because 3.8 is under 4.0. What is the right call?", options:["Fine: anything under the absolute maximum is guaranteed to work","Not acceptable: design inside the recommended 1.7–3.6 V range","Fine, if the typical column shows normal current at 3.8 V","Fine for short periods, since absolute maximums apply only to continuous use"], answer:1, why:"Absolute maximum ratings mark where damage begins, even briefly; they are never operating conditions. Operation is guaranteed only within the recommended conditions, so 3.8 V is out of spec. Typical values describe one sample at one condition and guarantee nothing."}
],
"p10m8":[
 {id:"p10m8q1", q:"A LoRa link runs SF8 at 125 kHz bandwidth with 4/5 coding. What are its symbol time and bit rate?", options:["1.024 ms; about 5.5 kb/s","2.048 ms; about 3.9 kb/s","4.096 ms; about 1.8 kb/s","2.048 ms; about 3.1 kb/s"], answer:3, why:"Symbol time = 2^8 ÷ 125,000 = 2.048 ms. Bit rate = 8 × (125,000 ÷ 256) × 0.8 ≈ 8 × 488 × 0.8 ≈ 3.1 kb/s. The 3.9 kb/s answer forgets the 4/5 coding rate, and 1.024 ms with 5.5 kb/s are the SF7 figures."},
 {id:"p10m8q2", q:"A US developer sets a LoRaWAN sensor to SF12 at 125 kHz with a 20-byte payload, reasoning that the slowest rate gives the most range. What is wrong with the plan?", options:["Nothing; adaptive data rate accepts SF12 whenever the link needs it","It breaks the US 400 ms dwell limit; SF10 is the slowest US uplink","SF12 is legal but breaks the 1% duty cycle, so it may send only once an hour","SF12 has worse sensitivity than SF7, so range would drop"], answer:1, why:"Under FCC 15.247 hybrid rules a transmission may dwell at most 400 ms per channel. SF12 at 125 kHz needs about 1.8 s for a 20-byte payload, so US915 uses SF10 with an 11-byte payload (371 ms) as its slowest uplink. The 1% duty cycle is a European rule."},
 {id:"p10m8q3", q:"A LoRaWAN sensor sends every 5 minutes at SF7: 72 ms of transmit at 45 mA plus about 3 mA·s for receive windows and sensing, and it sleeps at 5 µA. Roughly what does it use per day, and what is its theoretical life on 3,000 mAh?", options:["About 0.62 mAh per day; about 13 years","About 0.50 mAh per day; about 16 years","About 1,800 mAh per day; under two days","About 0.12 mAh per day; about 68 years"], answer:0, why:"Per uplink: 0.072 s × 45 mA ≈ 3.2 mA·s, plus 3 = 6.2 mA·s; × 288 a day ≈ 1,800 mA·s ≈ 0.50 mAh, plus 0.12 mAh of sleep = 0.62 mAh. 3,000 ÷ 0.62 ≈ 13 years. 0.50 forgets sleep; 1,800 mAh forgets to divide mA·s by 3,600."},
 {id:"p10m8q4", q:"A client buys Matter-over-Thread door sensors. Their phone commissions each one over Bluetooth, but afterwards the sensors are unreachable. Their only network gear is a Wi-Fi router. What is missing?", options:["A Zigbee coordinator stick, since Thread devices speak Zigbee once commissioned","A vendor cloud account, because Matter devices are controlled through the cloud","A Thread border router, such as a HomePod mini or a Pi with a Thread radio","A BLE hub, because Matter uses Bluetooth for all ongoing control"], answer:2, why:"Matter runs over IP, and Thread devices form an 802.15.4 IPv6 mesh that needs a border router to reach Wi-Fi or Ethernet. BLE is used only to commission. Zigbee shares the 802.15.4 radio but is a different network, and Matter control is local, not cloud-based."},
 {id:"p10m8q5", q:"A client's LoRaWAN sensors were set up with activation-by-personalization (ABP). After a power outage, the network server silently drops all their uplinks. What is the likely cause and fix?", options:["The AppKey expired during the outage; print a new key on each device label","Frame counters reset on reboot and look like replays; switch to OTAA","The gateway lost its Class C schedule; switch the sensors to Class C","The sensors rejoined on European 868 MHz channels after the reboot; reflash them for US915"], answer:1, why:"With ABP the session keys are static and frame counters restart at zero after a reboot, so the server treats new uplinks as replays and drops them. Over-the-air activation derives fresh session keys at each join. Root keys belong off device labels, not printed on them."}
]
});
Object.assign(window.LQ,{
"p54m1":[
 {id:"p54m1q1", q:"A managed IT client has 20 users at $90 per user. Tools cost $25 per user, labor averages 0.7 hours per user at a loaded $40 an hour, and winning the client cost $3,700. What are the gross margin and the CAC payback?", options:["About 72%; about 2.8 months","About 69%; about 3 months","About 41%; about 2 months","About 41%; about 5 months"], answer:3, why:"Revenue $1,800; tools 20 × $25 = $500; labor 14 h × $40 = $560; gross profit $740, a 41% margin. Payback = 3,700 ÷ 740 = 5 months. 72% leaves out the labor, the cost that most often hides, and dividing CAC by revenue instead of gross profit gives a flattering 2 months."},
 {id:"p54m1q2", q:"Your MRR is $4,200, overhead is $1,800 a month and your minimum draw is $3,500. What is your coverage ratio, and what would bring it to 1.0?", options:["0.79; about $1,100 more MRR, such as 14 more care plans at $79","2.33; it can already survive a month with no new projects","0.79; one $6,000 website project closes the gap for good","1.26; it is above 1.0, so the business is already safe"], answer:0, why:"Coverage = MRR ÷ (overhead + minimum draw) = 4,200 ÷ 5,300 ≈ 0.79, so about $1,100 more MRR reaches 1.0, roughly 14 care plans at $79. A website pays once and does nothing for next month, and 2.33 leaves your own draw out of fixed costs."},
 {id:"p54m1q3", q:"Solo, you bill $150,000 a year at a 30% margin. You hire a technician; together you bill $240,000, but margin falls to 25%. Using Maister's margin × productivity × leverage, what happens to your profit as the only partner?", options:["It falls, because fees per professional drop from $150,000 to $120,000","It falls, because margin drops from 30% to 25%","It stays the same, since leverage does not affect profit per partner","It rises from $45,000 to $60,000, because leverage doubles"], answer:3, why:"Solo: 0.30 × $150,000 × 1 = $45,000. With the hire: 0.25 × $120,000 × 2 = $60,000. Productivity and margin both fell, but leverage doubled, and the three factors multiply. Judging one factor alone, as the first two options do, misreads the identity."},
 {id:"p54m1q4", q:"A flat-fee managed IT client opens 40 tickets a month, most caused by unpatched machines it refuses to replace, and its margin has gone negative. Which response follows the lesson?", options:["Tighten scope and onboarding standards, and price per user","Move the client to break-fix so you earn more as things break","Absorb it, because a managed contract means unlimited support","Raise the flat fee 10% a month until the margin recovers, leaving the scope as it is"], answer:0, why:"The managed model puts the risk of a high-ticket client on you, and the defenses are a precise scope, onboarding standards that refuse to support an unpatched mess at a flat fee, and per-user pricing. Returning to break-fix revives the incentive to profit from failures."},
 {id:"p54m1q5", q:"Which offer earns the most contribution per owner-hour? Website: $4,800, $200 of costs, 40 hours. Care plan: $99 a month, $9 of costs, 30 minutes. AI receptionist: $399 a month, $120 of costs, 1.25 hours. Managed IT: $1,000 a month, $220 of tools, 8 hours.", options:["The website, at $4,600 of contribution","The care plan, at about $180 per hour","The AI receptionist, at about $223 per hour","Managed IT, which has the largest monthly fee"], answer:2, why:"Contribution per hour = (revenue − non-labor costs) ÷ hours: website $115, care plan $180, AI receptionist (399 − 120) ÷ 1.25 ≈ $223, managed IT $97.50. The largest fee or the biggest single check is not the most productive use of scarce owner hours."}
],
"p54m2":[
 {id:"p54m2q1", q:"Last quarter 50% of your leads took a discovery call, 60% of calls led to a proposal and 40% of proposals were signed. About how many leads a month do you need to add three clients a month?", options:["About 25","About 8","About 13","About 6"], answer:0, why:"Lead-to-client = 0.5 × 0.6 × 0.4 = 12%, so 3 ÷ 0.12 = 25 leads a month. Eight uses only the 40% close rate, and 13 starts counting at the discovery call; every stage of the funnel multiplies into the total."},
 {id:"p54m2q2", q:"A dental office gets 150 calls a week; 12% go unanswered, and about 40% of those callers book elsewhere. A new patient's first visit is worth about $250. What value at risk goes in your discovery summary?", options:["About $4,500 a week, roughly $19,500 a month","About $15,000 a week, roughly $65,000 a month","About $420 a week, roughly $1,800 a month","About $1,800 a week, roughly $7,800 a month"], answer:3, why:"150 × 0.12 = 18 unanswered calls; 40% of them, about 7.2, are lost; 7.2 × $250 ≈ $1,800 a week, about $7,800 a month. $4,500 assumes every unanswered caller is lost, and $15,000 applies the 40% to all calls instead of the unanswered ones."},
 {id:"p54m2q3", q:"A client's wish list prices at $14,000, but their budget is $9,000. What does the lesson recommend?", options:["Cut your price on the whole list by about a third so it fits","Prioritize with MoSCoW and fit the Musts into the budget","Accept the full list at $9,000 and recover the difference through later change orders","Decline the client, since a gap that size means a bad fit"], answer:1, why:"MoSCoW is how a $14,000 wish list fits a $9,000 budget without anyone feeling cheated: the Musts get built and the rest stays visible and priced. Discounting the whole list destroys margin, and planning to recover it through change orders starts the relationship with a hidden dispute."},
 {id:"p54m2q4", q:"A website project has stalled for three weeks because the client has not sent copy or photos, yet they insist the launch date is still yours to meet. Which SOW provisions would have prevented the dispute?", options:["A limitation-of-liability cap and an IP ownership clause","Explicit out-of-scope exclusions and two revision rounds","A 5-business-day acceptance period in which silence counts as acceptance","Dated client responsibilities, with milestones tied to client inputs"], answer:3, why:"Most delays start with client inputs. Listing content, access and decisions as client responsibilities and tying milestones to them means a late delivery moves the dates by the contract's own terms. An acceptance period ends projects but does nothing about inputs that never arrive."},
 {id:"p54m2q5", q:"A care-plan client sends two or three tiny out-of-scope requests a week: a font change, a new photo, a banner edit, each taking 10 to 20 minutes. What handles this best?", options:["Sell a prepaid bank of hours, drawn down and reported monthly","Write a separate signed change order for every request","Do them free to build goodwill, since each one is so small","Refuse every request that falls outside the plan's scope"], answer:0, why:"A prepaid bank keeps small changes priced without a form for every font change, and the monthly report shows what was used. Doing them free teaches the client that scope is negotiable, and a change order per ten-minute task costs more in paperwork than the work itself."}
],
"p54m3":[
 {id:"p54m3q1", q:"As a Texas sole owner you expect $70,000 of net self-employment earnings. Using the lesson's method, about how much self-employment tax should you budget?", options:["About $10,710, 15.3% of the full $70,000","About $8,680, the Social Security share only","About $9,891, 15.3% of 92.35% of earnings","About $4,945, since the employer half is deductible"], answer:2, why:"SE tax applies 15.3% (12.4% Social Security plus 2.9% Medicare) to 92.35% of net earnings: 70,000 × 0.9235 × 0.153 ≈ $9,891. $10,710 skips the 92.35% step. Half of the tax is deductible for income tax, but you still pay all of it."},
 {id:"p54m3q2", q:"A new client's file server died and they need it recovered tonight; nobody knows yet what failed or what backups exist. Which pricing model fits, according to the lesson?", options:["A fixed fee, so the client knows the cost up front","Value pricing based on what a day of downtime costs them","Per-device pricing, the way managed IT is billed","Hourly with a not-to-exceed cap, since the scope is unclear"], answer:3, why:"With unclear scope, hourly (time and materials) leaves the risk where the unknowns are, and a not-to-exceed cap protects the client. A fixed fee moves all the estimating risk to you before you know what failed; value pricing needs an outcome you can quantify and influence."},
 {id:"p54m3q3", q:"Two independent work packages have three-point estimates (O, M, P) of (8, 12, 22) and (10, 16, 28) hours. Using PERT and the 80th percentile (mean + 0.84σ), what fixed fee do you quote at $150 an hour?", options:["About 33 hours, about $5,000","About 30 hours, about $4,500","About 28 hours, about $4,200","About 50 hours, about $7,500"], answer:0, why:"Means: (8 + 48 + 22) ÷ 6 = 13 and (10 + 64 + 28) ÷ 6 = 17, total 30; σs 2.33 and 3.0 combine to √(5.4 + 9) ≈ 3.8; P80 ≈ 30 + 3.2 = 33.2 hours, about $5,000. Quoting the 30-hour mean means overrunning about half the time."},
 {id:"p54m3q4", q:"You sold an AI receptionist plan at $299 a month with 'unlimited minutes'. One client's volume reaches 4,000 minutes a month, at about $0.094 per minute all-in. What is the problem, and the fix?", options:["About $376 of usage exceeds the fee; sell included minutes plus overage","Usage costs about $37.60, so the plan is still very profitable","Model tokens are the main cost, so a cheaper LLM would fix the margin","Hosted services are the problem; local hardware is free once bought, so move the calls onto it"], answer:0, why:"4,000 × $0.094 ≈ $376 a month against a $299 fee, a loss before any tuning time. Voice components (telephony, speech and the platform fee), not tokens, dominate per-minute cost, so a cheaper model barely helps; included minutes plus overage tie revenue to usage."},
 {id:"p54m3q5", q:"You plan to run a dense 70B model at 4-bit (about 40 GB of weights) for live receptionist calls on a machine with 800 GB/s of memory bandwidth. Roughly what decode speed can the whole machine reach, and what happens with four calls at once?", options:["About 800 tokens a second, plenty for any number of callers","About 20 tokens a second for each caller, since each call gets the full bandwidth","About 40 tokens a second, because only GPU cores, not memory, limit decoding","About 20 tokens a second shared by all calls; too slow, so use an API"], answer:3, why:"Each generated token reads all the weights, so decoding tops out near 800 ÷ 40 = 20 tokens a second for the whole machine, shared by simultaneous callers. Local hardware must be sized for peak load; real-time voice belongs on an API, or on a small mixture-of-experts model with API fallback."}
],
"p54m4":[
 {id:"p54m4q1", q:"Last year your total federal tax was $14,000 on an AGI of $160,000. This year you expect to owe $20,000. What is the smallest equal quarterly estimated payment that still meets a safe harbor?", options:["$3,500 a quarter, 100% of last year's tax","$3,850 a quarter, 110% of last year's tax","$4,500 a quarter, 90% of this year's tax","$5,000 a quarter, all of this year's tax"], answer:1, why:"Because last year's AGI exceeded $150,000, the prior-year safe harbor is 110% of $14,000 = $15,400, or $3,850 a quarter. $3,500 uses the 100% rule, which applies only at lower incomes; 90% of this year's $20,000 is also safe but costs $4,500 a quarter."},
 {id:"p54m4q2", q:"A client's MSA for your $1,200-a-month managed IT service makes you liable without limit for all damages, including the client's lost profits. What is the sound response?", options:["Sign it, since your LLC shields your personal assets from any claim","Sign it, since your tech E&O policy covers whatever liability the contract assigns","Negotiate a 12-month-fees cap and exclude consequential damages","Strike the liability clause entirely so that neither side can claim anything"], answer:2, why:"Unlimited liability for a $1,200-a-month service is a bet-the-company term; the usual cap is 12 months of fees with indirect and consequential damages excluded. Policies often exclude liability taken on by contract beyond what you would owe anyway, and an LLC protects your house, not the business itself."},
 {id:"p54m4q3", q:"You invoice a San Antonio client $1,000 a month as one line, 'IT services', covering hosting, consulting and hands-on computer repair. What is the risk, and the right step?", options:["It may be taxed as its most taxable part; itemize and confirm each line","No risk: Texas does not tax services sold by IT firms","Tax automatically applies to only 80% of the invoice because it includes hosting","Because the tools are cloud-hosted, only the client's home-state rules apply"], answer:0, why:"Repair of tangible property is taxable in full and data processing on 80%, while consulting may be treated differently, so a bundle on one line may be taxed as its most taxable part. Itemizing, and getting each service line's treatment in writing, is the lesson's step."},
 {id:"p54m4q4", q:"Your RMM account is compromised and ransomware is pushed to three client networks. Which coverage combination responds both to your own response costs and to the clients' claims?", options:["General liability, which covers any damage you cause","Commercial auto, since you drive to the client sites","Professional liability alone, because it was a failure in your work","First-party cyber, plus third-party cyber and tech E&O"], answer:3, why:"General liability covers bodily injury and property damage, not data loss. First-party cyber pays your own breach response; third-party cyber and E&O answer claims from clients whose systems were hit through you. E&O alone leaves your own response costs uncovered."},
 {id:"p54m4q5", q:"A single-member LLC owner pays his personal mortgage from the business account, has no company agreement, and personally guaranteed the office lease. A client then sues over a failed migration. Which statement is accurate?", options:["The LLC protects him from every claim because it is properly filed with the state","He is personally liable on the lease, and commingling can void the LLC's shield","The lease is protected because it is in the LLC's name, whatever he guaranteed","Only an S-corporation election would protect his personal assets"], answer:1, why:"An LLC does not cover debts you personally guarantee or your own negligence, and commingling personal and business money is how courts come to disregard it. A separate account and a written company agreement are the evidence that the LLC is a real, separate business."}
],
"p54m5":[
 {id:"p54m5q1", q:"A client's site depends on a host at 99.95%, a CDN at 99.99% and your application stack at 99.5%. What is the best-case availability of the chain, and can you sign a 99.5% SLA on it?", options:["99.5%, the weakest link, so a 99.5% SLA is exactly safe","About 99.81%, the average of the three, so 99.5% has room to spare","About 99.44%, so a 99.5% SLA is not supported","99.99%, set by the strongest component"], answer:2, why:"Dependencies multiply: 0.9995 × 0.9999 × 0.995 ≈ 0.9944, about 4.1 hours down in a 30.44-day month, already worse than 99.5% before any of your own mistakes. Neither the weakest link nor the average describes a chain in which any one failure takes the site down."},
 {id:"p54m5q2", q:"Your SLO is 99.9% a month, an error budget of about 43.8 minutes. By day 9 you have used 31 minutes. A major plugin upgrade is planned for day 12. What does the error budget tell you to do?", options:["Go ahead, since 12.8 minutes remain, more than the upgrade should take","Freeze risky changes like the upgrade and fix the cause","Issue service credits now, because the SLO has been breached","Lower the SLO to 99.5% for the rest of the month so the upgrade fits"], answer:1, why:"With most of the budget spent by day 9, the rule is to stop spending it: freeze risky changes and fix what consumed it. Nothing has been breached yet, and credits follow the SLA, not the SLO. Going ahead bets the remaining 12.8 minutes on an upgrade."},
 {id:"p54m5q3", q:"A client's 900 GB file server must be restored within an 8-hour RTO. The only backup is offsite, reachable over a 200 Mbit/s link. What does the arithmetic say?", options:["About 1.25 hours, comfortably inside the RTO","About 4.5 hours, leaving time to rebuild","About 10 hours, but the RTO starts only after the data arrives, so it is met","About 10 hours, so the RTO needs a local copy"], answer:3, why:"900 GB × 8 = 7,200 Gbit, and 7,200 ÷ 0.2 Gbit/s = 36,000 s, about 10 hours, already past the 8-hour RTO before any rebuild. The 1.25-hour answer forgets to convert bytes to bits. RTO is the maximum time to restore service, transfer included."},
 {id:"p54m5q4", q:"Which of these alerts should page you at 2 a.m., rather than open a ticket for the morning?", options:["The external check shows a client's site returning 503","A server's disk has reached 86% and is still climbing slowly","A client's TLS certificate is due to expire in 21 days","A workstation is missing one non-critical monthly patch"], answer:0, why:"Page only on user-visible symptoms that need a human now, and a site returning 503 is exactly that. The disk, certificate and patch alerts are real but can wait as tickets; paging on them trains you to ignore the phone when it matters."},
 {id:"p54m5q5", q:"Which step in this client offboarding plan is a mistake?", options:["Deliver documentation, configurations and a final backup export with checksums","Hand over all credentials to the client, then rotate them","Keep your RMM agents and admin access for 90 days, just in case","Start the domain transfer early, because registrar transfer locks can last 60 days"], answer:2, why:"Offboarding removes your access: delegated admin in the tenant, RMM agents, your accounts and API keys. Leaving a skeleton key in a former client's network is the risk the 2021 Kaseya attack showed. The other steps, including starting domain transfers early, are what the lesson prescribes."}
],
"p54m6":[
 {id:"p54m6q1", q:"A dental practice's office manager emails 'go ahead and scan our network and website' for a security assessment. The website sits on a shared web host. What do you need before any testing starts?", options:["Nothing more; the manager's email is written permission","Written authorization from an officer, naming testers, systems and dates","A verbal go-ahead from the dentist on a recorded call, added to the email","Nothing more if you already run the practice's IT, since you know the systems"], answer:1, why:"Authorization must come in writing from someone with authority over the specific systems, normally an officer, and name the testers, systems and dates. A client cannot authorize testing of its host's shared infrastructure. Running their IT adds an independence issue to disclose, not authority."},
 {id:"p54m6q2", q:"Your offsite backup vendor would store a medical practice's ePHI encrypted, without holding the key, but it refuses to sign a BAA. May you use it for that data?", options:["Yes: encrypted data without the key falls outside HIPAA","Yes, as long as the practice has signed a BAA with you, which covers your vendors","Yes, if you agree to report any breach to the practice within 60 days","No: storing ePHI makes it a business associate, BAA required"], answer:3, why:"HHS guidance is explicit that a cloud provider storing ePHI is a business associate even when it holds only encrypted data without the key. The chain runs downstream: your BAA with the practice requires subcontractor BAAs from your vendors; it is not a substitute for them."},
 {id:"p54m6q3", q:"A client wants to take cards on the website you host, and also wants its AI receptionist to take card numbers over the phone. Which design follows the lesson?", options:["Store cards encrypted on the web server so repeat customers can check out faster","Let the receptionist capture card numbers and delete the transcripts every night","Use the processor's hosted payment page, and keep cards off the calls","Collect cards on your own form and forward them to the processor's API, since TLS protects them"], answer:2, why:"A hosted page or embedded field keeps card numbers off the client's server, keeping the merchant on the shortest questionnaire and your hosting largely out of scope. A single recorded or transcribed card number pulls the whole call system into PCI scope, nightly deletion or not."},
 {id:"p54m6q4", q:"A San Antonio client's AI receptionist takes calls from Florida and California. The owner says Texas is one-party consent, so the greeting needs no recording notice. What do you advise?", options:["Disclose recording and automation on every call, for every state","Agree; the business is in Texas, so Texas law governs every call","Disclose only to callers whose caller ID shows an all-party-consent state","Skip the notice but turn on voiceprint identification to log who consented"], answer:0, why:"Florida and California require every party's consent, and with callers from many states the safe design assumes the strictest law; continuing after a clear notice is how consent is usually obtained. Caller ID does not prove location, and voiceprints add Texas biometric-consent duties."},
 {id:"p54m6q5", q:"A law firm wants a real-time voice intake agent and a document assistant over its confidential client files. Which recommendation usually survives scrutiny?", options:["Run both locally, live voice included, on a single box for maximum privacy","Hybrid: documents on local hardware, live voice on a cloud API under a DPA","Use consumer AI apps for both, since they offer the best models","Use cloud APIs for both with no agreements, since business APIs never retain data"], answer:1, why:"Private, steady document work fits local hardware that keeps files under the firm's control, while live voice needs cloud elasticity and redundancy; one local box is a single point of failure. Cloud use still needs a signed DPA and checked retention settings, and consumer app terms often differ."}
],
"p54m7":[
 {id:"p54m7q1", q:"You plan to hire a $48,000-a-year technician in Texas. Add employer FICA (7.65%), FUTA ($42), Texas unemployment tax (2.7% of the first $9,000), a $4,800 health stipend, $2,400 of tools, $1,200 of training and $500 for workers' comp. At 75% of 1,840 hours, what is the loaded cost per billable hour?", options:["$48,000 loaded, about $35 per billable hour","About $51,700 loaded (salary plus FICA), about $37 per billable hour","About $60,900 loaded, about $44 per billable hour","About $60,900 loaded, about $33 per billable hour"], answer:2, why:"48,000 + 3,672 + 42 + 243 + 4,800 + 2,400 + 1,200 + 500 ≈ $60,860, and 75% of 1,840 is 1,380 billable hours, so about $44 an hour. The $33 answer divides by all available hours, but only billable hours earn revenue."},
 {id:"p54m7q2", q:"Which plan for gathering reviews complies with the FTC's 2024 rule and Google's review policy?", options:["Send every client a review link at go-live and answer every review","Offer a $50 account credit for each five-star review posted","Send the review link only to clients who scored 9 or 10 on your satisfaction survey","Have your subcontractor post a review describing the work as a satisfied client"], answer:0, why:"Asking every client at a moment of visible success, and answering all reviews without disclosing client details, is the honest path. Rewards tied to sentiment and undisclosed insider reviews violate the FTC rule, and asking only happy clients breaks Google's policy against selective solicitation."},
 {id:"p54m7q3", q:"Your utilization has been 82% for four months and you are turning work away. Your pipeline covers 1.2 times a technician's salary, and cash covers two months of it. What does the lesson's hiring test say?", options:["Hire now: utilization above 80% is the signal that matters","Subcontract for now: the pipeline and cash tests both fail","Bring on a 'contractor' who works only for you, on your schedule and tools, to avoid payroll costs","Push utilization to 95% before deciding anything"], answer:1, why:"Hire when utilization, pipeline and cash all agree; here two of the three fail, so a variable-cost subcontractor relieves the load without risking runway. A 'contractor' you control like an employee is probably an employee, and misclassification brings back taxes and penalties."},
 {id:"p54m7q4", q:"You have $12,000 in receivables on $18,000 of revenue a month (use a 30-day month). What is your days sales outstanding, and what does the lesson suggest to lower it?", options:["About 45 days; move clients to net-30 terms to bring it down","About 67 days; send reminders only once invoices pass 60 days","About 30 days, one billing cycle, whatever the receivables","About 20 days; autopay on recurring invoices would cut it"], answer:3, why:"DSO = receivables ÷ revenue × days = 12,000 ÷ 18,000 × 30 = 20 days. The 45-day answer inverts the ratio. Autopay on recurring invoices collects on the due date, which is how the model agency planned to cut its DSO from 24 days to under 15."},
 {id:"p54m7q5", q:"Option A: a solo firm with subcontractors earns the owner $120,000 on 40-hour weeks. Option B: a five-person firm pays the owner $130,000 for 55-hour weeks. Both run 48 weeks a year. Which is better by profit per owner-hour?", options:["B, because $130,000 is the larger figure","B, because more staff means more leverage and so more profit per hour","A, at about $62.50 per owner-hour against about $49","They are equal, since the two incomes are within 10%"], answer:2, why:"A: 120,000 ÷ (40 × 48 = 1,920 hours) ≈ $62.50 an hour. B: 130,000 ÷ (55 × 48 = 2,640 hours) ≈ $49. Here the extra hours cost more than the extra pay, which is why the lesson measures profit per owner-hour rather than income or headcount."}
]
});
